  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Rhode Island received $753M in federal contract obligations, fiscal year to date. Obligations fell 16.9% year over year. The state recorded 4,142 awards, with an average award size of $182K. The Department of Defense obligated $609M, making the Department of Defense the largest buyer in the state so far this fiscal year. SEACORP, LLC received $110M, making SEACORP, LLC the top vendor by obligations. Engineering Services is the largest industry by obligations, at $274M. Fiscal-year comparisons show variability: FY21 was $928M, FY22 was $1.2B, FY23 was $1B, FY24 was $1.2B, FY25 was $906M, and FY26 is $753M. These figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date. Buyers beyond the Department of Defense include the Department of Veterans Affairs at $53.3M and the General Services Administration at $37M. Other industry concentrations include Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $97M and Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing at $71.4M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in Rhode Island changed recently?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 16.9% year over year. Total obligations are $753M fiscal year to date, compared with prior years of $928M in FY21, $1.2B in FY22, $1B in FY23, $1.2B in FY24, and $906M in FY25. Award velocity shows 4,142 awards so far, with an average award size of $182K. These totals are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the main buyers and suppliers of federal contracts in Rhode Island?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $609M, making the Department of Defense the largest buyer. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $53.3M and the General Services Administration obligated $37M. SEACORP, LLC received $110M and RAYTHEON COMPANY received $86.1M, making SEACORP, LLC and RAYTHEON COMPANY the top vendors; Engineering Services led industries at $274M."
